| 832 | on the compiled path, a generic dotted method whose single parameter has a tuple type, written or from a tuple static argument, dies at the call with `ClassCastException` in `castTo`, under the bound `Object` and the bound `Any` alike: `Box[\ZZ32\](1).first[\ZZ32\]((2, 3))`; walk runs it | NEGATIVE-VERIFIED | implementation gap (codegen) | `basic/expressions/method-invocation.tex`, "Dotted Method Invocations" | none | the bound-Any compiled probe | siblings: 339. Program, compiled and run (`explorations/perf-probes/bound-any-compiled/REPORT.md`): `object Box[\T\](x: T) first[\U\](y: (U, U)): U = do (a, c) = y; a end end`. The compile command's bound `Any` lets a tuple static argument reach it: `b.wrap[\(ZZ32,ZZ32)\]((2, 3))` for `wrap[\U\](y: U)` dies alike. `CodeGen.receiverPrependedDomainType` (`ProjectFortress/src/com/sun/fortress/compiler/codegen/CodeGen.java:6529-6542` at c124c6b65) prepends the receiver to a tuple domain's elements, so cannot tell one tuple-typed parameter from several. The call casts to a three-place arrow; the loader makes a two-place closure. Workaround: two parameters, as `second[\U\](y: U, z: U)`. |
| 833 | walk cannot run the compiled checker on its programs, as P-12's step after the switch-over needs: walk runs with type checking and the Scala checker off, the Java checker is gone, and walk has no case for the checker's `CoercionInvocation` nodes, so it cannot take the static coercions | NEGATIVE-VERIFIED | implementation gap (walk) | `Specification/basic/conversions-coercions.tex`, "Coercion Resolution" | `ProjectFortress/tests/XXXCoercionStaticRungC.fss` | the foundations review's tenets evidence | siblings: 69. `walk` calls `setScala(false)` and leaves `fortress.compile.typecheck` false, its default (`ProjectFortress/src/com/sun/fortress/Shell.java:422-424`, `:1277` at c124c6b65). With type checking on and Scala off, `StaticChecker.checkCompilationUnit` throws 'The Java version of the type checker is gone now.' (`ProjectFortress/src/com/sun/fortress/compiler/StaticChecker.java:229`). `grep -rl CoercionInvocation` over `interpreter/` finds no file. By reading; not run. With the checker on, walk would also meet its open departures (rows 470, 731) and the other components' errors (F-290). The reproducer is walk's run-time choice, P-59's limit until then. |
| 514 | the compiled path has no strided range: `seq(0:10:3)` is refused by the checker, "(ZZ32, ZZ32)->Range is not applicable to an argument of type (Range, IntLiteral)", while walk runs it (4 elements) | NEGATIVE-VERIFIED | library gap vs spec (prelude) | `Specification/basic/expressions/ranges.tex`, "Ranges" | `ProjectFortress/compiler_tests/XXXStridedRangeRungT.fss` | climb batch N rung T | siblings: 661, 71, 74, 314. The compiler library declares `opr :(lo:ZZ32, hi:ZZ32)` and `opr #(lo:ZZ32, sz:ZZ32)` only (`Library/CompilerLibrary.fsi:171-172`, at c124c6b65), so `0:10:3` parses as `(0:10):3`; with a `nat` stride `0:10:n` the same. The one library declares the strided `:` at `Range[\ZZ32\]`, which is no generator, so the checker refuses `seq(a:b:c)` against it too (row 661). The test is a `compile` `.test` pinned by `compile_err_contains`. Workaround: a `#` or `:` range and a stride computed in the body. full text: history, row 514 |
